<h1>Agent Parameters</h1>

<p>
    Parameters are the <strong>pieces of information an agent needs</strong> to do its job. Examples include the client, policy, contact, amount, or date range. You can think of them as the blanks in a form that the agent fills in for your email, link, or workflow.
</p>

<!-- IMAGE PLACEHOLDER: Screenshot of the agent designer "Parameters" table with example rows for Client, Policy, Contact Email, and Due Date. Show the table with columns for Display Name, Type, Required, Default Value, and Hidden/Visible. -->

<h2>What Are Parameters?</h2>

<p>
    Parameters define what information your agent needs before it can run. When someone triggers your agent, they either:
</p>

<ul>
    <li><strong>Provide the information</strong> – Enter values in a dialog or chat</li>
    <li><strong>Pull it from context</strong> – The system automatically gets it from the current page (which client is open, which policy is selected, etc.)</li>
</ul>

<p>
    For example, a "Send Renewal Reminder" agent might need:
</p>

<ul>
    <li><strong>Client</strong> – Which client to send the reminder to</li>
    <li><strong>Policy</strong> – Which policy is renewing</li>
    <li><strong>Contact Email</strong> – Where to send the email</li>
    <li><strong>Due Date</strong> – When the renewal is due</li>
</ul>

<!-- IMAGE PLACEHOLDER: Visual example showing a "Send Renewal Reminder" agent with its parameters listed. Show how these parameters are used when the agent runs—perhaps showing them being filled in a dialog or email template. -->

<h2>Required vs. Optional</h2>

<p>
    Parameters can be either required or optional:
</p>

<ul>
    <li><strong>Required parameters</strong> – Must have a value before the agent can run (for example, client or main contact). The agent won't start without these.</li>
    <li><strong>Optional parameters</strong> – Nice to have but not strictly necessary (for example, note to the underwriter or CC recipient). The agent can run without these, using default values if provided.</li>
</ul>

<p>
    <strong>Best practice:</strong> Use required parameters for anything your email template or action cannot work without. Make everything else optional to keep agents flexible.
</p>

<!-- IMAGE PLACEHOLDER: Screenshot showing required vs. optional parameters in the parameter list. Use visual indicators (asterisks, checkmarks, etc.) to show which are required. Show an example of an agent that won't run because a required parameter is missing. -->

<h2>Parameter Types</h2>

<p>
    Choose a type that matches what the user should enter:
</p>

<ul>
    <li><strong>Text</strong> – Names, notes, or short descriptions
        <ul>
            <li>Use for: Client names, custom messages, notes</li>
            <li>Example: "Welcome message" parameter</li>
        </ul>
    </li>
    <li><strong>Number</strong> – Amounts, counts, or percentages
        <ul>
            <li>Use for: Premium amounts, policy counts, percentages</li>
            <li>Example: "Premium amount" parameter</li>
        </ul>
    </li>
    <li><strong>Date</strong> – Effective dates, due dates, or time periods
        <ul>
            <li>Use for: Renewal dates, effective dates, deadlines</li>
            <li>Example: "Renewal due date" parameter</li>
        </ul>
    </li>
    <li><strong>Toggle / Yes-No</strong> – Boolean options like "Include certificates?" or "Mark as urgent"
        <ul>
            <li>Use for: Yes/no choices, feature toggles, options</li>
            <li>Example: "Include attachments?" parameter</li>
        </ul>
    </li>
    <li><strong>Client</strong> – Reference to a client record
        <ul>
            <li>Use for: Selecting which client the action applies to</li>
            <li>Example: "Client" parameter</li>
        </ul>
    </li>
    <li><strong>Policy</strong> – Reference to a policy record
        <ul>
            <li>Use for: Selecting which policy the action applies to</li>
            <li>Example: "Policy" parameter</li>
        </ul>
    </li>
</ul>

<p>
    Where it makes sense, set a <strong>default value</strong> so common choices are pre-filled and users only adjust when needed.
</p>

<!-- IMAGE PLACEHOLDER: Close-up of a parameter edit panel showing fields for Display Name, Type dropdown, Required toggle, Default Value, and Hidden/Visible toggle. Show examples of different parameter types being configured. -->

<h2>Hidden vs. Visible Parameters</h2>

<p>
    Not every parameter needs to be shown to the user. Some values can be pulled from the page automatically.
</p>

<h3>Hidden Parameters</h3>
<ul>
    <li>Filled in behind the scenes from the current context</li>
    <li>Examples: Current client ID, selected renewal, primary contact email</li>
    <li>User never sees these—they're automatically populated</li>
    <li>Use to keep the run dialog clean and avoid re-entering information the system already knows</li>
</ul>

<h3>Visible Parameters</h3>
<ul>
    <li>Appear in the run dialog so users can review or type a value</li>
    <li>Examples: Custom message, specific date, amount to include</li>
    <li>User sees and can edit these before the agent runs</li>
    <li>Use when the user needs to provide input or confirm a value</li>
</ul>

<p>
    <strong>Tip:</strong> Use hidden parameters for data that's always available from the page context. Use visible parameters when the user needs to make a choice or enter custom information.
</p>

<!-- IMAGE PLACEHOLDER: Side-by-side comparison showing an agent run dialog with all visible parameters vs. the same agent with most parameters hidden. Show how hidden parameters make the dialog cleaner and easier to use. -->

<h2>Using Presets and Context</h2>

<p>
    When adding parameters, you can often start from <strong>presets</strong> that already know how to pull data from the page:
</p>

<ul>
    <li><strong>Current Client</strong> – Automatically gets the client from the current page</li>
    <li><strong>Selected Renewal</strong> – Gets the renewal that's currently selected</li>
    <li><strong>Primary Contact Email</strong> – Gets the main contact's email for the current client</li>
    <li><strong>Policy Number</strong> – Gets the policy number from the current context</li>
    <li>Other presets may be available depending on the context</li>
</ul>

<p>
    This keeps your agents consistent and reduces setup mistakes. Presets are pre-configured to pull the right data automatically.
</p>

